<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Apis bull</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apis was a sacred bull worshipped at Memphis in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as the living manifestation of the god Ptah. A single bull with particular markings was chosen for the role, and on its death it was mummified and buried with great ceremony in the Serapeum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>. Greek writers accused the Persian kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a> of killing an Apis bull, stories that may reflect hostile propaganda.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apis.jpg" alt="Apis" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PharaohCrab · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apis_god.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
